  <blockquote>
    <p>
      Life is not what one lived, but what one remembers and how one remembers it in order to
      recount it.
    </p>
    <cite>Gabriel García Márquez</cite>
  </blockquote>

  <div class="mag__body">
    <p class="mag__lead">
      When you tune your radio to a Peruvian frequency, the first sound that greets you might be the
      ethereal, distant, and cold notes of the Andean panpipe (Siku). It is a cultural remnant of the
      Inca Empire, a melody belonging to high altitudes and soaring condors.
    </p>
    <p>
      Yet, in the lowlands along the Pacific coast, hidden among sea-swept fishing villages and former
      sugar plantations, exists another Peruvian sound—one that does not play to the sky, but strikes
      the earth. This is Afro-Peruvian music, long forgotten by orthodox history, a rhythm born in
      suffering that bred profound joy and resilience.
    </p>

    <h2>The Artist: A “Scavenger” of History — Susana Baca</h2>
    <p>
      To truly understand this hidden sound, you cannot bypass a woman whose name itself sounds like
      poetry: Susana Baca.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you listen to her sing for the first time, you will be captivated by its texture. Her voice is
      not soaring; instead, it carries the raspy, unpolished grit of bare feet walking on warm sand.
      There is an unrefined grace in her singing—as if she isn't performing on a stage, but whispering
      to you beside an old grandmother's bonfire.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a long time, Peru's Afro-descendant communities were marginalized and silenced. In her
      youth, rather than catering to mainstream pop, Susana and her husband embarked on a lifelong
      “roots journey” akin to anthropologists. They drove along the coast, delving into remote Black
      villages, recording the oral ballads, fading drumbeats, and ancestral memories of the elders.
    </p>
    <p>
      She spent decades salvaging these historical fragments, breathing new life into them with her
      voice. Not only did she reintroduce the magnificent Afro-Peruvian heritage to the world, but in
      2011, she also became the first Black cabinet minister (Minister of Culture) in the history of
      independent Peru. With her voice, she erected a spiritual monument for her people.
    </p>
  </div>

  <figure class="mag__figure" style="--fig-pos: 50% 15%">
