    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="mag__body">
    <h2>The Instrument: Cajón — A Heartbeat Born in Confinement</h2>
    <p>
      When listening to Afro-Peruvian music, your body is involuntarily drawn to a highly textured
      percussion. The instrument looks incredibly primitive: it is simply a wooden box. It is called
      the Cajón (meaning “crate” or “drawer”).
    </p>
    <p>
      The birth of the Cajón is a history of blood, tears, and resistance. Spanish colonizers brought
      enslaved Africans to coastal plantations in Peru. To prevent the slaves from using drumbeats to
      send signals for assembly and rebellion, the colonizers strictly forbade them from owning or
      playing traditional African hand drums.
    </p>
    <p>
      But the human desire for rhythm cannot be stripped away. Deprived of their drums, the slaves
      turned to the most common items around them—discarded wooden shipping crates used for papayas or
      fish. During their brief moments of rest from hard labor, they sat on these crates and slapped
      different parts of the wood with calloused hands to simulate high and low drumbeats.
    </p>
    <p>
      The crate became the new drum; the cargo container became a resonant chamber for the soul. The
      suffering and oppression of the past eventually transformed into the passionate, light, and
      dance-inducing rhythms on the wooden box. Today, the Cajón has crossed borders to become an
      indispensable percussion instrument in global unplugged music and Flamenco.
    </p>

    <h2>Listen Now: Encounters in the Soundscape</h2>
    <p>
      This is the true magic of World Music—behind every rhythm stands a living community and their
      weighty history. Now, let us return to our continental tour. On the map of South America, we
      have handpicked several Peruvian stations for you, each one checked against its official domain
      and stream metadata. Click to listen:
    </p>
